 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "Lut, his guests, his people and the people of al-Ayka are never drawn; nothing of the punishment is drawn (no stones, no overturned city, no sun at the blast). Geometric only on «واتقوا الله». 71 «هؤلاء بناتي» is glossed only with As-Sa'di's «من شدة الأمر الذي أصابه». 72 addresses the Prophet ﷺ: no plate; both tafsirs read «لعمرك» as Allah's oath by his life. Not glossed: 74:1-6 and 79:1-2 (the tafsirs' wording is in Allah's first person or repeats «عذاب يوم الظلة»). Light for the signs (75), footsteps for «لبسبيل مقيم». STAGING v4: Restaged under Staging v4: Lut is a blue drop labelled Lut, his people an uncountable faceless crowd in the town. His guests, his daughters and his wife are not drawn. 68-70: Lut speaks (geometric only on 69:2, the Name), they answer and shake their heads. 71-72: Lut speaks, they turn away (As-Sa'di: they paid no heed); 72:1 addresses the Prophet: not shown, no plate. 73-74: the town is empty (no person on stage): the blast on 73:2, the ruin event on 74:2 (the closest event to the town turned upside down), stones on 74:6; time is day, not dawn (no dawn over a punishment), although 73:3 is sunrise. 75-77: a traveller passes the ruins on the road (Al-Muyassar: the travellers who pass by see them). 78-79: the people of al-Ayka (uncountable) in a garden (As-Sa'di: a garden of many trees), they turn away and leave the stage on 78:5, so 79:1 falls on the empty garden (a leaf plate); no punishment event (the verse names none); Shu'ayb is not drawn (not named in these verses); a traveller on the clear road (79:3-5). The garden setting also has a stream (decor, not in the sources)."
 },
